        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Not tested hands-on; picked from listings, specs and owner reviews. We did not walk or run on these machines
            or listen to their motors. We read each Amazon listing through Amazon’s product API on 24 November 2026,
            checked makers’ own figures where this site has already recorded them, and compared both with our existing
            brand pages. Every figure on the page traces back to one of those sources.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four bars decided the list. <strong>The listing had to state a brushless motor</strong> in its title or
            feature bullets. <strong>It had to come from a named brand</strong>; dozens of unbranded walking pads now
            claim brushless motors, and we passed over those whose listings give no brand or support beyond the
            listing. <strong>Its basic figures had to be stated</strong>: speed, weight limit and incline at minimum,
            with belt size where the listing gives it. And <strong>it had to be the best brushless option for one kind
            of buyer</strong>, so that the seven picks cover different budgets and uses rather than seven versions of
            the same walking pad.
          </p>
          <p>
            We judged them first as treadmills and only second as brushless ones. A brushless motor in a machine with a
            short deck, a low weight limit or no warranty does not make it a good buy, so the usual criteria (belt size,
            weight limit, speed range, incline and warranty) carried most of the weight. We gave little weight to
            decibel figures and lifespan claims in listings, which we cannot check, and none to star ratings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-brushless-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Does a Brushless Treadmill Motor Mean?</h2>
<p>
            Almost every home treadmill uses a DC motor. In a conventional brushed DC motor, two carbon blocks called
            brushes press against a rotating commutator and carry current to the spinning windings. The brushes are a
            wear part: they slowly shorten, shed carbon dust and eventually need replacing, and their contact with the
            commutator adds a little friction, heat and noise. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor brushes</a> covers what wear looks like and how they are replaced.
          </p>
          <p>
            A brushless DC motor removes the brushes and the commutator. The magnets sit on the rotor, the windings stay
            still, and an electronic controller switches current between the windings in sequence, using sensors or
            feedback to know where the rotor is. The result keeps the DC motor’s fine speed control and compact size while
            removing the main mechanical wear part. As our <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC vs DC motor guide</a> puts it, brushless is a genuine feature but not a rating.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Question</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brushed DC</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brushless DC</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wear parts in the motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushes and commutator wear; bearings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bearings only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Routine motor maintenance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Occasional brush check or replacement</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None beyond keeping it clean and cool</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor noise</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slight brush hiss on top of motor hum</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor hum without brush noise</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Simpler speed control board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">More complex electronic controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Typical failure</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn brushes, then controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller or sensor fault</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tells you the power?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The last row is the one to remember. Neither label tells you how much load a motor can sustain. That depends
            on its continuous rating, its cooling and the controller that drives it, and those are the figures most
            budget listings leave out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-it-quieter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Brushless Treadmill Quieter?</h2>
<p>
            The motor is, slightly. The treadmill as a whole often is not, by as much as the listing suggests. Removing
            the brushes removes their hiss and some of the high-pitched whine, which is noticeable when you stand next to
            the motor hood. But most of what you and your neighbours hear from a treadmill in use is not the motor. It is
            your feet landing on the deck, the belt passing over the rollers and deck, and the vibration of the whole
            frame travelling into the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why the decibel figures on these listings deserve caution. MERACH claims under 25 dB for its W50
            walking pad, and UMAY, LONTEK and others claim under 45 dB. None says at what speed, under what load or at what
            distance the figure was measured, and we could not check any of them. A walking pad at 2 mph with a light
            walker will be quiet whatever the motor; a jogger at 6 mph on a hard floor will be heard downstairs whatever
            the motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            If quiet is the reason you are shopping, a brushless motor is a reasonable box to tick, but it should come
            after the things that make a bigger difference: walking rather than jogging, a thick rubber mat, a ground-floor
            room and a well-lubricated belt. Our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a> covers what neighbours actually hear, and the <a href="/best-quiet-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best quiet walking pads</a> page lists the quietest under-desk options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="does-it-last-longer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does a Brushless Motor Last Longer?</h2>
<p>
            It removes one reason a motor wears out, which is a real advantage. Brushes are the part most likely to need
            attention in a brushed DC motor over the years, and a brushless motor does not have them. Bearings still
            wear, windings can still overheat if the motor is overloaded, and the electronic controller still has to last.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some listings go much further. UREVO says the CyberMega’s motor has a lifespan ten times longer than
            traditional brushed motors and quotes 620,000 miles of training; UREVO’s CyberPad listing quotes a motor
            lifespan of at least 6,000 hours; Sole says the F85’s motor delivers up to ten times longer life. Those are
            the makers’ own claims and we cannot verify them. The figure that tells you what a maker will actually stand
            behind is the warranty, and only Sole on this page backs its brushless motor with long, published cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice most treadmill motors, brushed or not, outlast the belt, the deck and often the owner’s interest.
            Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmill motors last</a> explains why overheating, a dry belt and an overloaded motor end more motors than brush wear does. A brushless
            motor in a walking pad that is routinely run at its weight limit will not last because it is brushless.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade-off worth knowing is repair. When a brushed motor’s brushes wear, they are a cheap, replaceable part.
            When a brushless motor fails, the fault is more likely to be in the controller or a sensor, which can mean a
            board replacement. With an online-only brand, that depends on the seller still stocking the part.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horsepower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does Brushless Change the Horsepower That Matters?</h2>
<p>
            No. The horsepower question is the same as for any treadmill: is the figure a peak or a continuous rating?
            Five of the seven Amazon listings here give a horsepower figure, from 2.5 HP on the Sunny Auto Incline to 4.0 HP on the
            UMAY and UREVO, and none on Amazon is labelled as continuous. On a $289 compact treadmill or a $187 walking
            pad, a 3 or 4 HP figure is almost certainly a peak. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains why a continuous rating is the number to compare and why peak figures on budget listings run high.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushless motors are often more efficient than brushed ones of similar size, which is part of why makers like
            them for compact walking pads: they can produce a usable output in a small, cool package. Sole’s F85 listing
            claims 15% greater energy efficiency for its brushless motor. That helps a motor run cooler and quieter, but it
            does not change what the deck, frame and weight limit can take.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Your use</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What matters more than brushless</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick here</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slow walking at a desk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low start speed, quiet deck, size</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">MERACH W50</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steep incline walking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline range, frame stability, handrails</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LONTEK, UMAY, Sunny Auto Incline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking and some jogging</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt width and length, weight limit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sunny Auto Incline, Sunny Wi-Fi</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Regular running</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck length, continuous rating, warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F85, UREVO CyberMega</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
