        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill with a brushless motor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most homes, the Sunny Health & Fitness Auto Incline Treadmill: a 2.5 HP brushless motor, 10 mph, 15% auto incline and a 20 by 53-inch surface, delivered fully assembled, for about $598 on 24 November 2026. For running, the UREVO CyberMega (about $900) and Sole F85 (about $2,000) are the brushless options.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a brushless treadmill motor better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In two ways: it has no carbon brushes to wear out, and it removes brush noise. It is not more powerful by definition, and when it fails the fault is more likely to be the controller than a cheap brush. Compare belt size, weight limit and warranty first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are brushless treadmills quieter?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The motor is a little quieter, but most treadmill noise comes from footfall, the belt and frame vibration. A brushless walking pad at walking pace will be quiet; any treadmill used for jogging on an upstairs floor will be heard. A rubber mat and a ground-floor room help more than motor type.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do brushless treadmill motors last longer?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They remove the brushes, which are the main wear part in a brushed DC motor, so they should need less maintenance. Claims such as ten times the lifespan are makers’ own and unverified. The motor warranty is the best guide to how long a maker expects it to last.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best brushless walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The MERACH W50, with a 3.5 HP brushless motor, 12% auto incline, 4 mph and a 400 lb limit for about $256 on 24 November 2026. For steeper walking with handrails, LONTEK’s 32% manual-incline pad costs about $187.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I know if a treadmill really has a brushless motor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check that the maker, not just the listing title, states it, and look for a motor warranty to match. Listings for unbranded walking pads use the word loosely, and this site has seen a brushless claim disputed by an independent teardown. If it matters to you, ask the seller in writing.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

